2:T5cb,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"TechArticle","headline":"The Pentagon's Blacklist Gamble: Why Adding Alibaba, Baidu, and BYD to the Military List Is Accelerating the Very Decoupling It Seeks to Prevent","description":"On June 8, 2026, the Pentagon added Alibaba, Baidu, BYD, and NIO to its Chinese Military Companies list. By June 24, Alibaba had sued the U.S. government. The blacklist now covers 188 entities—a 45% expansion in one year. But the real story isn't the legal fight. It's how Washington's escalating pressure is forcing China's tech giants to build a parallel ecosystem that may soon need no American technology at all.","image":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa?w=1200&q=80","datePublished":"2026-06-25T16:00:00.000Z","dateModified":"2026-06-25T16:00:00.000Z","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Meeeeed@AI in China","url":"https://www.ainchina.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"AI in China","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.ainchina.com/logo.svg"}},"mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.ainchina.com/blog/pentagon-blacklist-alibaba-baidu-byd-china-tech-decoupling-2026/"},"keywords":"[\"Pentagon blacklist\", \"Chinese Military Companies list\", \"Alibaba lawsuit\", \"BYD\", \"Baidu\", \"NIO\", \"US China tech decoupling\", \"Section 1260H\", \"China tech sanctions\", \"military-civil fusion\", \"Huawei\", \"AI chips\", \"China semiconductor independence\"]"}5:["slug","pentagon-blacklist-alibaba-baidu-byd-china-tech-decoupling-2026","d"]
0:["n8V4HI3bdOAeSri5wnB7-",[[["",{"children":["blog",{"children":[["slug","pentagon-blacklist-alibaba-baidu-byd-china-tech-decoupling-2026","d"],{"children":["__PAGE__?{\"slug\":\"pentagon-blacklist-alibaba-baidu-byd-china-tech-decoupling-2026\"}",{}]}]}]},"$undefined","$undefined",true],["",{"children":["blog",{"children":[["slug","pentagon-blacklist-alibaba-baidu-byd-china-tech-decoupling-2026","d"],{"children":["__PAGE__",{},[["$L1",[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$2"}}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"minHeight":"100vh","backgroundColor":"#0a0a0a","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","nav",null,{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"16px 24px"},"children":["$","div",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":"1200px","margin":"0 auto","display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":"24px"},"children":[["$","$L3",null,{"href":"/","style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","textDecoration":"none","fontWeight":"700","fontSize":"18px"},"children":"AI in China"}],["$","$L3",null,{"href":"/blog/","style":{"color":"#a3a3a3","textDecoration":"none","fontSize":"14px"},"children":"Blog"}]]}]}],["$","article",null,{"style":{"maxWidth":"800px","margin":"0 auto","padding":"48px 24px"},"children":[["$","header",null,{"style":{"marginBottom":"48px"},"children":[["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","gap":"12px","marginBottom":"24px","flexWrap":"wrap"},"children":[["$","span",null,{"style":{"backgroundColor":"#1a1a1a","padding":"6px 12px","borderRadius":"6px","fontSize":"12px","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee","textTransform":"uppercase","letterSpacing":"0.05em"},"children":"AI Policy"}],["$","span",null,{"style":{"backgroundColor":"#1a1a1a","padding":"6px 12px","borderRadius":"6px","fontSize":"12px","color":"#737373"},"children":"15 min read"}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"style":{"fontSize":"36px","fontWeight":"700","lineHeight":"1.2","margin":"0 0 24px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":"The Pentagon's Blacklist Gamble: Why Adding Alibaba, Baidu, and BYD to the Military List Is Accelerating the Very Decoupling It Seeks to Prevent"}],["$","div",null,{"style":{"display":"flex","alignItems":"center","gap":"16px","color":"#737373","fontSize":"14px"},"children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"June 26, 2026"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"·"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"AI in China"}]]}]]}],["$","img",null,{"src":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa?w=1200&q=80","alt":"The Pentagon's Blacklist Gamble: Why Adding Alibaba, Baidu, and BYD to the Military List Is Accelerating the Very Decoupling It Seeks to Prevent","style":{"width":"100%","height":"auto","borderRadius":"12px","marginBottom":"48px"}}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","img","0",{"src":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589829085413-56de8ae18c73?w=800&q=80","alt":"A desk with legal documents, old books, and a gavel symbolizing the intersection of international law and technology regulation","style":{"width":"100%","borderRadius":"12px","margin":"24px 0"}}],["$","p","1",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"*The Pentagon's expanding blacklist now covers 188 Chinese entities, but the designation may be accelerating the very technological independence Washington seeks to prevent. Photo: Unsplash*"}]]}],["$","p","3",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"On the morning of June 8, 2026, the U.S. Department of Defense published an updated roster of Chinese Military Companies that would have been unthinkable in scope just two years earlier. The list—formally known as Section 1260H under the National Defense Authorization Act—had grown from roughly 130 entities to "}],["$","strong","1",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"188"}],["$","span","2",{"children":". The new additions were not obscure defense contractors or state-owned shipbuilders. They were household names: "}],["$","strong","3",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Alibaba"}],["$","span","4",{"children":", China's largest e-commerce and cloud computing giant; "}],["$","strong","5",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Baidu"}],["$","span","6",{"children":", the search engine that has pivoted aggressively into AI and autonomous driving; "}],["$","strong","7",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"BYD"}],["$","span","8",{"children":", the world's biggest electric vehicle manufacturer; and "}],["$","strong","9",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"NIO"}],["$","span","10",{"children":", another major EV maker. Also included: "}],["$","strong","11",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Unitree"}],["$","span","12",{"children":", the robotics company whose humanoid robots had just made headlines at global tech conferences, and "}],["$","strong","13",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"WuXi AppTec"}],["$","span","14",{"children":", a biotechnology firm with deep ties to American pharmaceutical companies."}]]}],["$","p","5",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Sixteen days later, on June 24, Alibaba filed a federal lawsuit in San Jose, California, challenging its designation. The complaint was direct and forceful: \"The determinations have no basis in fact or law.\" BYD had already threatened similar legal action. WuXi AppTec had sued on June 11. China's Ministry of Commerce, in a statement on June 13, warned that Beijing would \"resolutely and forcefully retaliate.\""}]]}],["$","p","7",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The legal battle is newsworthy. But the deeper story is structural. The Pentagon's blacklist—designed to slow China's technological advance by restricting access to American defense contracts and signaling broader market risks—appears to be achieving the opposite of its intended effect. Every major Chinese tech company now on the list is accelerating its shift to domestic suppliers, domestic software ecosystems, and domestic capital markets. The designation that was meant to contain China's tech ecosystem is becoming the accelerant that completes its independence."}]]}],["$","h2","9",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"What the Blacklist Actually Does—and Doesn't"}]]}],["$","p","11",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"To understand the stakes, one must first understand the mechanics. Section 1260H of the NDAA, created by congressional mandate in 2021, requires the Secretary of Defense to publish an annual list of Chinese companies the Pentagon believes have ties to the People's Liberation Army. The legal consequences are more nuanced than a straightforward sanctions regime. Being listed does "}],["$","strong","1",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"not"}],["$","span","2",{"children":" automatically trigger export controls, asset freezes, or criminal penalties. It does something more subtle—and, in some ways, more consequential."}]]}],["$","p","13",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Direct impact:"}],["$","span","1",{"children":" Starting June 30, 2026, the Pentagon cannot enter into new contracts with listed companies or their controlled subsidiaries. By June 2027, the prohibition extends to third-party procurement—meaning the U.S. government cannot acquire goods or services from these companies even through intermediaries."}]]}],["$","p","15",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Secondary impact:"}],["$","span","1",{"children":" The designation functions as a signal to investors, partners, and allied governments. Institutional investors increasingly treat the 1260H list as a precursor to more restrictive measures. Lobbying firms, fearful of reputational risk, drop clients. Supply chain managers scrub vendor lists. American companies that source from or partner with listed firms begin to look for alternatives."}]]}],["$","p","17",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Limited immediate legal consequences:"}],["$","span","1",{"children":" Unlike the Commerce Department's Entity List—which can cripple a company by cutting off access to American technology—the 1260H list does not automatically ban sales or technology transfers. Alibaba can still sell goods on Amazon. BYD can still sell cars in Europe. Baidu can still license its AI models. The weapon is reputational and anticipatory, not prohibitive."}]]}],["$","p","19",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"This distinction is why the Chinese companies have chosen to fight in court rather than simply accept their fate. Alibaba's lawsuit, filed in the Northern District of California, argues that the designation is \"arbitrary and capricious,\" violates the company's First Amendment rights by restricting its ability to retain lobbying firms, and has caused \"irreparable harm\" to its reputation and business relationships. The complaint notes that Alibaba's board is independent, its shareholders include major American financial institutions like JPMorgan, Citigroup, and BlackRock, and its products are \"built for retail, logistics, and enterprise information technology—not weapons, defense, or intelligence.\""}]]}],["$","div","28",{"style":{"overflowX":"auto","margin":"24px 0"},"children":["$","table",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","borderCollapse":"collapse","fontSize":"14px"},"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Section 1260H List Expansion"}]]}],["$","th","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"2024"}]]}],["$","th","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"2025"}]]}],["$","th","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"June 2026"}]]}],["$","th","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Change"}]]}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Total entities listed"}]]}],["$","td","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"~70"}]]}],["$","td","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"~130"}]]}],["$","td","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"188"}]]}],["$","td","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"+45%"}]]}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Major tech/EV additions"}]]}],["$","td","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"DJI, SenseTime"}]]}],["$","td","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"CATL, BGI"}]]}],["$","td","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Alibaba, Baidu, BYD, NIO, Unitree, TP-Link"}]]}],["$","td","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}]]}],["$","tr","2",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Direct DoD contracting ban"}]]}],["$","td","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Active"}]]}],["$","td","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Active"}]]}],["$","td","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Active June 30, 2026"}]]}],["$","td","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}]]}],["$","tr","3",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Third-party procurement ban"}]]}],["$","td","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}],["$","td","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}],["$","td","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"June 2027"}]]}],["$","td","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Companies that have sued"}]]}],["$","td","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"0"}]]}],["$","td","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"0"}]]}],["$","td","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Alibaba, WuXi, (BYD threatening)"}]]}],["$","td","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"—"}]]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","p","29",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"*Sources: Federal Register, Department of Defense, Reuters, company filings.*"}]]}],["$","h2","31",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The Companies Fighting Back"}]]}],["$","h3","33",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Alibaba: The Principal Gateway to China"}]]}],["$","p","35",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Alibaba's legal challenge is the most significant because the company's relationship with American business is the deepest. In its complaint, Alibaba describes itself as \"the principal gateway to the Chinese market\" for many American businesses. The designation, it argues, \"directly impugns Alibaba's reputation and casts a shadow over every U.S. relationship the company maintains.\""}]]}],["$","p","37",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The impact is already measurable. According to the lawsuit, \"advocates who have represented Alibaba for years have informed the company that they can no longer do so.\" Lobbying firms, which serve as critical conduits for navigating Washington's regulatory landscape, are dropping the company preemptively—before any formal sanctions exist. The chill effect operates through anticipation, not prohibition."}]]}],["$","p","39",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Alibaba's stock reacted with unusual volatility. On June 9, the day after the list was published, Alibaba's Hong Kong-listed shares fell 0.5%. Baidu dropped 2.3%. BYD declined 0.5%. NIO, which had risen over 2% earlier in the day, gave back gains. These moves were relatively modest, suggesting that investors—like the companies themselves—have begun to price in the expectation that U.S.-China tech decoupling is inevitable rather than exceptional."}]]}],["$","h3","41",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"BYD: The World's Largest EV Maker Refuses to Comply"}]]}],["$","p","43",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"BYD's response has been the most aggressive. Stella Li, BYD's executive vice-president, stated publicly that the company would use every legal \"weapon\" at its disposal to fight the designation. The company has not merely threatened a lawsuit; it has framed the blacklist as a competitive weapon aimed at hobbling BYD because of its international success."}]]}],["$","p","45",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The Pentagon's justification for BYD's inclusion is that the company is \"directly and indirectly affiliated\" with SASAC (China's State-owned Assets Supervision and Administration Commission) and the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology. BYD is described as a \"military-civil fusion contributor to the Chinese defence industrial base.\" The Pentagon has provided no direct evidence for these claims."}]]}],["$","p","47",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The stakes for BYD are particularly high because the company operates a manufacturing facility in Lancaster, California, and is actively expanding into European and Southeast Asian markets. A 1260H designation doesn't block these operations, but it creates friction. American fleet buyers, state procurement agencies, and institutional investors may hesitate. BYD's legal strategy is to challenge the designation before that hesitation crystallizes into permanent exclusion."}]]}],["$","h3","49",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Baidu and the AI Dimension"}]]}],["$","p","51",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Baidu's inclusion carries special weight for the AI industry. Unlike Alibaba or BYD, Baidu is not primarily a consumer or automotive company. It is an AI research lab with one of China's largest search engines, an autonomous driving division (Apollo), and a large language model (ERNIE) that competes directly with OpenAI and Google. The Pentagon's designation of Baidu as a military company sends a signal that Washington views Chinese AI capabilities as inherently threatening—regardless of their civilian application."}]]}],["$","p","53",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Baidu's response was measured but firm. The company stated that the suggestion it is a military company is \"entirely baseless.\" Unlike Alibaba, Baidu has not yet filed a lawsuit, but industry observers expect the company to follow Alibaba's lead if the designation remains in place."}]]}],["$","p","55",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The broader question is what happens to Baidu's AI partnerships. The company has research collaborations with American universities, supplies AI technology to international automakers, and operates the ERNIE Bot platform with 220 million monthly active users. A 1260H designation doesn't sever these ties, but it introduces a risk premium that partners must price into their calculations."}]]}],["$","div","65",{"style":{"overflowX":"auto","margin":"24px 0"},"children":["$","table",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","borderCollapse":"collapse","fontSize":"14px"},"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Company"}]]}],["$","th","1",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Pentagon Justification"}]]}],["$","th","2",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Company Response"}]]}],["$","th","3",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Stock Impact (Jun 9)"}]]}],["$","th","4",{"style":{"borderBottom":"2px solid #22d3ee","padding":"12px 16px","textAlign":"left","fontWeight":"600","color":"#22d3ee"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"US Operations"}]]}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"children":[["$","td","0",{"style":{"borderBottom":"1px solid #1a1a1a","padding":"12px 16px","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Alibaba"}]]}],[